{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalParamAnchorReal","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalParamAnchorReal","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalParam","lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.HoefsmitRep"],"declarations":1,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.Seminormal.S1_eq_seminormalParam","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.Seminormal.S1_eq_seminormalParam","kind":"theorem","line":34,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalParamAnchorReal.lean#L34-L66","doc":"**Anchor.**  The hand-built `(2,1)` matrix `S1` — constructed in `HoefsmitRep.lean` to\nsatisfy the braid relation with `S0`, independently of any axial-distance formula — is the\ngeneric block at the parameter this file predicts.  In `[[1,2],[3]]` the entry `2` has\ncontent `1` and `3` has content `-1`, so `σ_1` acts with axial distance `-2`.\n\nThe hypothesis `q² ≠ 1` is necessary, not cosmetic: at `q = ±1` the numerator `q - q⁻¹`\nand the denominator `1 - q⁴` both vanish, so `seminormalParam q (-2)` evaluates to `0`\n(Lean's `0 / 0`), while `S1`'s `(0,0)` entry is `-1 / (2q) ≠ 0`.  That is the Hecke\nalgebra degenerating to the group algebra, where the seminormal form is not given by this\nformula. ","signatureChunk":0}]}